{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966801,"nonce":"TPMxhPil+n8hxLYzTwBSmmJmGjM=","title":"ScrollPhysics::update_scrollbar_visibility recreated the opacity spring every frame,","message":"`ScrollPhysics::update_scrollbar_visibility` (in [scroll.rs](crates/blinc_layout/src/widgets/scroll.rs)) used to `scheduler.remove_spring(old); scheduler.add_spring(new)` every time it was called — and `tick_scrollbar` calls it on every Scrolling auto-detect tick and every FadingOut transition. Each `add_spring` fires `notify_active` → wake_callback → `frame_dirty=true`, so the windowed runner's Phase 5 frame gate kept passing at ~7 Hz forever, pinning CPU at ~30 % even when nothing visually changed.\n\n**Why:** The user reported \"30 % CPU since interaction and never drops\" on cn_demo. Backtrace under `RUST_LOG=blinc_anim::notify_active=trace` (one-off backtrace added during diagnosis) showed `add_spring → update_scrollbar_visibility → tick_scrollbar → tick_scroll_physics` firing 67 times in 10 s.\n\n**How to apply:** Any per-frame state-machine tick that calls into the scheduler must early-return when its target hasn't actually moved. The fix:\n\n```rust\nif (self.scrollbar_target_opacity - target).abs() \u003c f32::EPSILON {\n    return;\n}\n```\n\ndropped `notify_active` from 67 → 2 fires / 10 s. Same anti-pattern to watch for anywhere a tick handler ends in `scheduler.add_*` without checking whether the parameters have changed since last frame.\n\nSee also [[gotcha-fast-path-visible-anim-latch]].","files":null}]}